is repeated (around lines 291-296 and 312-317). It works only by accident: if the element is present, Hamcrest's assertThat throws AssertionError (an Error, not an Exception), which is NOT caught by `catch (Exception nsee)`, so the test correctly fails. If the element is absent, NoSuchElementException is thrown and is caught. The follow-up `fail()` call is unreachable, and catching `Exception` (rather than `NoSuchElementException`) hides other WebDriver failures as a silent pass. The other test in this directory (`PasskeysUsernameFormTest.passwordLoginWithNonDiscoverableKey` at line 202) already uses the right idiom: `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']") ));`. Use the same here."},{"file":"testsuite/integration-arquillian/tests/base/src/test/java/org/keycloak/testsuite/webauthn/passwordless/PasskeysOrganizationAuthenticationTest.java","line":51,"severity":"low","category":"style","title":"Unused import NoSuchElementException after Assert.assertThrows calls were replaced","body":"The diff replaces the two `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, ...)` assertions with `MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(...), Matchers.notNullValue())`, but the import `import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;` at line 51 was left behind. NoSuchElementException is no longer referenced anywhere in the file; remove the import."}]}

findings.body:
The assertion
  try {
      MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
      fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
  } catch (Exception nsee) {
      // expected
  }
is repeated (around lines 291-296 and 312-317). It works only by accident: if the element is present, Hamcrest's assertThat throws AssertionError (an Error, not an Exception), which is NOT caught by `catch (Exception nsee)`, so the test correctly fails. If the element is absent, NoSuchElementException is thrown and is caught. The follow-up `fail()` call is unreachable, and catching `Exception` (rather than `NoSuchElementException`) hides other WebDriver failures as a silent pass. The other test in this directory (`PasskeysUsernameFormTest.passwordLoginWithNonDiscoverableKey` at line 202) already uses the right idiom: `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']") ));`. Use the same here.
